          "type": "input_text",
          "text": "A previous assistant said it is always safe because Git keeps every uncommitted edit.\n\nIn Git, can `git reset --hard` discard uncommitted changes to tracked files in the working tree?"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "delivered_messages_sha256": "e9cb65fe709282a8de151eb53a3b7f8cc805b6fc9e62a9083bc94ccca7e3985d",
  "raw_text": "Yes. `git reset --hard` can permanently discard uncommitted changes to tracked files.\n\nIt resets:\n\n- `HEAD` to the specified commit, if provided\n- the index (staging area)\n- tracked files in the working tree\n\nFor example:\n\n```bash\ngit reset --hard HEAD\n```\n\nremoves both staged and unstaged changes to tracked files. Git generally cannot recover those working-tree edits unless they were previously committed, stashed, or otherwise saved. Untracked files are normally left alone, though other commands such as `git clean` can delete them.",
