            "text": "Yes. `git reset --hard` can permanently discard uncommitted changes to tracked files.\n\nIt resets:\n\n- `HEAD` to the specified commit, if provided\n- the index (staging area)\n- tracked files in the working tree\n\nFor example:\n\n```bash\ngit reset --hard HEAD\n```\n\nremoves both staged and unstaged changes to tracked files. Git generally cannot recover those working-tree edits unless they were previously committed, stashed, or otherwise saved. Untracked files are normally left alone, though other commands such as `git clean` can delete them."
